-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part wra3 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-04; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- Welfare Reform Act 2012 (United Kingdom), 2012 c. 5 -- part wra3 -- THE SEAT LAYER.
-- Written from inputs/ukpga-2012-5.txt, a plain-text rendering of legislation.gov.uk (version 2026-07-01), not the official text.
--
-- SEAT: the individual who claims or receives personal independence payment (Part 4), who is party to a child support case
-- (Part 6), or who is affected by the schedules this part covers. Nothing here is new law: each question phrases a rule
-- already encoded and tested in the layer-1 modules imported above, and calls it. No rule is restated.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS. Every question takes yes/no, numbers, dates and the modules' own choices, one for each fact the person can
-- state, each with an @desc worded as the person would be asked. The records the rules expect (Person) are built inside each
-- question, in its WHERE, from those answers; a field that no rule called reads is given a fixed placeholder, with a comment.
--
-- A FACT THE PERSON MAY NOT BE ABLE TO STATE. Several rules take a MAYBE input: what regulations provide, what a decision-maker
-- has decided, a figure left to regulations. A person cannot state "what regulations prescribe" as a bare yes or no, and a
-- bare yes or no would turn "not yet settled" into "no". So each such fact is TWO plain inputs, always asked together:
-- "do you know whether ...?" and "...?". The WHERE joins them with the helpers below: known gives JUST the answer, not known
-- gives NOTHING, and NOTHING reaches the rule, which keeps it NOTHING. The second answer is ignored when the first is no.
--
-- RESULTS. Every question returns a MAYBE value and returns the rule's result unchanged. JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are the
-- text's own yes and no; NOTHING means the text does not decide the case, or leaves it to regulations or another Act that is
-- not among the sources. A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE. Read each question's @desc for the scope its answer claims.
--
-- QUESTIONS NOT ASKED, and why (each is encoded in layer 1 and has a coverage row in NOTES.md):
--   * the answer would be the person's own input handed back: the weekly rates (ss 78(3), 79(3), 82(6)), the prescribed
--     activities (ss 78(4), 79(4)), "prescribed" (s 95), the housing credit amount (Sch 4, SPCA 3A(3));
--   * the provision only restates what the person already knows or defines a term for another provision (s 77(2), the s 81(2)
--     and (3) definitions that the section 81 questions use, the care home and qualifying-service definitions in s 85(3) to (7),
--     the s 84(2) instruments, the s 92(3) definitions, the s 95 definitions, Sch 12 definitions);
--   * the provision addresses only an official and gives the person nothing: s 88(3), (4), s 89 (the report to Parliament),
--     s 135(2) and (3), s 136(1)(a), s 139, s 141 (the review of fees regulations), s 148, Schedule 13 (the Social Mobility and
--     Child Poverty Commission) and Schedule 14 (repeals);
--   * Schedule 4 paragraph 7 and 12, which only say how housing credit is construed;
--   * a limb of a list that another part also adds a limb to, which the lead composes at the merge (Schedule 9 paragraph 46,
--     the two limbs (ca) of section 6A(1) of the Social Security Fraud Act 2001; paragraph 47, limb (be) of section 10(3);
--     paragraph 31, limb (ad) of section 187(1) of the Social Security Administration Act 1992): the host lists' lead-ins
--     are not printed, so no limb alone answers a question the person could ask.
-- WHICH RATE. The questions under sections 78(1), (2) and 79(1), (2) apply only their own conditions; the questions under sections
-- 78(6) and 79(6) combine them with the other provisions on entitlement, once for either rate, once for the standard rate and once
-- for the enhanced rate (section 82 displaces section 78(1) and (2) for a terminally ill claimant, and drops the required period
-- condition for the mobility component). They all take the same inputs, with the same wording, so one form can answer all six.
-- The Schedule 4 questions are marked "assumed, not ruled": state pension credit housing credit is adjacent to the seat's core
-- questions and is included so that the schedule is not silently left out.

§ `Helpers: a fact the person may be unable to state`

-- The person can say, or cannot. If they can, the fact is JUST what they say; if not, it is NOTHING and the rule keeps it so.
GIVEN `the person can say` IS A BOOLEAN
      `what the person says` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` MEANS
    IF `the person can say` THEN JUST `what the person says` ELSE NOTHING

GIVEN `the person can say` IS A BOOLEAN
      `what the person says` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`wra3 seat a number the person may be unable to give` MEANS
    IF `the person can say` THEN JUST `what the person says` ELSE NOTHING

GIVEN `the person can say` IS A BOOLEAN
      `what the person says` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`wra3 seat a date the person may be unable to give` MEANS
    IF `the person can say` THEN JUST `what the person says` ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 4 -- Personal independence payment`

§§ `Section 77 -- Personal independence payment`

@desc Only the section 77(3) bar. JUST TRUE means this bar does not apply to you; it does not say you are entitled. NOTHING means the conditions are left to regulations and have not been settled for you.
@export Under section 77(3), am I free of any bar for want of the prescribed residence and presence conditions?
GIVEN `I know whether I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you meet the conditions about residence and presence in Great Britain that the regulations set? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 77(3), am I free of any bar for want of the prescribed residence and presence conditions` IS
    `s 77(3) the person is not barred for want of the prescribed residence and presence conditions` `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set` `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set`

@desc Only the section 77(4) bar. JUST FALSE means you are barred while you are entitled to any of the four Scottish payments the section names.
@export Under section 77(4), am I free of any bar from being entitled to a Scottish disability payment?
GIVEN `I am entitled to Scottish adult disability living allowance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to Scottish adult disability living allowance?
      `I am entitled to pension age disability payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to pension age disability payment?
      `I am entitled to adult disability payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to adult disability payment?
      `I am entitled to child disability payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to child disability payment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 77(4), am I free of any bar from being entitled to a Scottish disability payment` IS
    `s 77(4) the person is not barred by being entitled to a Scottish disability payment` `the Scottish disability payments I am entitled to`
    WHERE
        `the Scottish disability payments I am entitled to` MEANS
            concat (LIST (IF `I am entitled to Scottish adult disability living allowance` THEN LIST `s 95 Scottish adult disability living allowance` ELSE EMPTY),
                         (IF `I am entitled to pension age disability payment` THEN LIST `s 95 pension age disability payment` ELSE EMPTY),
                         (IF `I am entitled to adult disability payment` THEN LIST `s 95 adult disability payment` ELSE EMPTY),
                         (IF `I am entitled to child disability payment` THEN LIST `s 95 child disability payment` ELSE EMPTY))

§§ `Section 78 -- Daily living component`

@desc Only the section 78(1) conditions. It does not apply the other provisions of Part 4 that section 78(6) makes this subject to; the question under section 78(6) does.
@export Under section 78(1), do I satisfy the conditions for the daily living component at the standard rate?
GIVEN `my ability to carry out daily living activities is limited by my physical or mental condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the assessment find that your ability to carry out daily living activities is limited by your physical or mental condition?
      `I know whether I meet the required period condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I meet the required period condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you meet the "required period condition"? (The question under section 81 asks what it turns on.) (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 78(1), do I satisfy the conditions for the daily living component at the standard rate` IS
    `s 78(1) the person is entitled to the daily living component at the standard rate` `my ability to carry out daily living activities is limited by my physical or mental condition` `I meet the required period condition, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `I meet the required period condition, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I meet the required period condition` `I meet the required period condition`

@desc Only the section 78(2) conditions. It does not apply the other provisions of Part 4 that section 78(6) makes this subject to; the question under section 78(6) does.
@export Under section 78(2), do I satisfy the conditions for the daily living component at the enhanced rate?
GIVEN `my ability to carry out daily living activities is severely limited by my physical or mental condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the assessment find that your ability to carry out daily living activities is severely limited by your physical or mental condition?
      `I know whether I meet the required period condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I meet the required period condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you meet the "required period condition"? (The question under section 81 asks what it turns on.) (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 78(2), do I satisfy the conditions for the daily living component at the enhanced rate` IS
    `s 78(2) the person is entitled to the daily living component at the enhanced rate` `my ability to carry out daily living activities is severely limited by my physical or mental condition` `I meet the required period condition, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `I meet the required period condition, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I meet the required period condition` `I meet the required period condition`

-- Section 78(6): "This section is subject to the provisions of this Part, or regulations under it, relating to entitlement to the daily living component".
@desc The section 78(1), (2) and 82(2) result at either rate, combined with the other Part 4 provisions on entitlement that this seat covers: sections 77(3), 77(4), 83(1) and 84(1). It does not cover the bars on payment in sections 85 to 88 (those have their own questions) or any provision of regulations that is not among the sources. NOTHING means a regulation-dependent fact has not been settled; it is never a no.
@export Under section 78(6), am I entitled to the daily living component?
GIVEN `I know whether I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you meet the conditions about residence and presence in Great Britain that the regulations set? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I am entitled to Scottish adult disability living allowance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to Scottish adult disability living allowance?
      `I am entitled to pension age disability payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to pension age disability payment?
      `I am entitled to adult disability payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to adult disability payment?
      `I am entitled to child disability payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to child disability payment?
      `my ability to carry out daily living activities is limited by my physical or mental condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the assessment find that your ability to carry out daily living activities is limited by your physical or mental condition?
      `my ability to carry out daily living activities is severely limited by my physical or mental condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the assessment find that your ability to carry out daily living activities is severely limited by your physical or mental condition?
      `I know whether I meet the required period condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I meet the required period condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you meet the "required period condition"? (The question under section 81 asks what it turns on.) (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I suffer from a progressive disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you suffer from a progressive disease?
      `the date I ask about` IS A DATE @desc On what date do you ask whether you are terminally ill? (day, month and year)
      `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected` IS A DATE @desc By what date can your death in consequence of the disease reasonably be expected? (If it is not expected in the foreseeable future, give a date far ahead, such as 1 January 2100.)
      `I have made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground of terminal illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground that you are terminally ill?
      `my age in years` IS A NUMBER @desc How old are you, in completed years?
      `I have reached pensionable age within the meaning of the Pensions Act 1995` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you reached pensionable age, as the rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the Pensions Act 1995 work it out?
      `I know whether regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations give an exception to the bar on entitlement after the relevant age that covers you? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `a relevant EU Regulation applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does Council Regulation (EC) No 1408/71 or Regulation (EC) No 883/2004 apply to you?
      `the United Kingdom is competent to pay me sickness benefits in cash for the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc During the period, is the United Kingdom the competent state for paying you sickness benefits in cash (for the purposes of Chapter 1 of Title III of the Regulation that applies)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 78(6), am I entitled to the daily living component` IS
    `s 78(6) the person is entitled to the daily living component, subject to the other provisions of Part 4 relating to entitlement`
        `my own result under section 78(1) or (2), or section 82(2)`
        (LIST `s 77(3) the person is not barred for want of the prescribed residence and presence conditions` `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set, as far as I know`,
              `s 77(4) the person is not barred by being entitled to a Scottish disability payment` `the Scottish disability payments I am entitled to`,
              `s 83(1) the person is not barred by having reached the relevant age` `me` `I have reached pensionable age within the meaning of the Pensions Act 1995` `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me, as far as I know`,
              `s 84(1) the person is not barred from the daily living component by the United Kingdom not being the competent state` `a relevant EU Regulation applies to me` `the United Kingdom is competent to pay me sickness benefits in cash for the period`)
    WHERE
        `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set` `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set`
        `I meet the required period condition, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I meet the required period condition` `I meet the required period condition`
        `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me` `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me`
        `the Scottish disability payments I am entitled to` MEANS
            concat (LIST (IF `I am entitled to Scottish adult disability living allowance` THEN LIST `s 95 Scottish adult disability living allowance` ELSE EMPTY),
                         (IF `I am entitled to pension age disability payment` THEN LIST `s 95 pension age disability payment` ELSE EMPTY),
                         (IF `I am entitled to adult disability payment` THEN LIST `s 95 adult disability payment` ELSE EMPTY),
                         (IF `I am entitled to child disability payment` THEN LIST `s 95 child disability payment` ELSE EMPTY))
        `I am terminally ill at the date I ask about` MEANS
            `s 82(4) the person is terminally ill at the time` `I suffer from a progressive disease` `the date I ask about` `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected`
        `section 82 applies to me` MEANS
            `s 82(1) this section applies to the person` `I am terminally ill at the date I ask about` `I have made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground of terminal illness`
        `my result at the standard rate, section 82 displacing section 78(1)` MEANS
            `s 82(2) the result under section 78(1), section 82 displacing section 78(1)` `section 82 applies to me`
                (`s 78(1) the person is entitled to the daily living component at the standard rate` `my ability to carry out daily living activities is limited by my physical or mental condition` `I meet the required period condition, as far as I know`)
        `my result at the enhanced rate, section 82 displacing section 78(2)` MEANS
            `s 82(2) the person is entitled to the daily living component at the enhanced rate, section 82 displacing section 78(2)` `section 82 applies to me`
                (`s 78(2) the person is entitled to the daily living component at the enhanced rate` `my ability to carry out daily living activities is severely limited by my physical or mental condition` `I meet the required period condition, as far as I know`)
        `my own result under section 78(1) or (2), or section 82(2)` MEANS
            `s 78(6) the disjunction of the results` (LIST `my result at the standard rate, section 82 displacing section 78(1)`, `my result at the enhanced rate, section 82 displacing section 78(2)`)
        `me` MEANS Person WITH
                `reference` IS "claimant"                    -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads the reference
                `age in years` IS `my age in years`
                `date of birth` IS Date 1 1 1900              -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads the date of birth
                `where the person is` IS `Greater London`     -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads where the person is
                `where the person lives` IS `Greater London`  -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads where the person lives

-- Section 78(6): "This section is subject to the provisions of this Part, or regulations under it, relating to entitlement to the daily living component".
@desc The section 78(1) result (section 82 displacing it for a terminally ill claimant), combined with the other Part 4 provisions on entitlement that this seat covers: sections 77(3), 77(4), 83(1) and 84(1). It does not cover the bars on payment in sections 85 to 88 (those have their own questions) or any provision of regulations that is not among the sources. NOTHING means a regulation-dependent fact has not been settled; it is never a no.
