            `s 78(6) the disjunction of the results` (LIST `my result at the standard rate`, `my result at the enhanced rate`)

-- Section 79(6): "This section is subject to the provisions of this Part, or regulations under it, relating to entitlement to the mobility component (see in particular sections 82 and 83)".
@desc The section 79(2) result, combined with the other Part 4 provisions on entitlement that this seat covers: sections 77(3), 77(4), 83(1) and 79(7). The required period condition is dropped for a person to whom section 82 applies (section 82(3)). It does not cover the bars on payment in sections 86 to 88 (those have their own questions) or any provision of regulations that is not among the sources. NOTHING means a regulation-dependent fact has not been settled; it is never a no.
@export Under section 79(6), am I entitled to the mobility component at the enhanced rate?
GIVEN `I know whether I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you meet the conditions about residence and presence in Great Britain that the regulations set? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I am entitled to Scottish adult disability living allowance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to Scottish adult disability living allowance?
      `I am entitled to pension age disability payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to pension age disability payment?
      `I am entitled to adult disability payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to adult disability payment?
      `I am entitled to child disability payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you entitled to child disability payment?
      `my age in years` IS A NUMBER @desc How old are you, in completed years?
      `I know the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the standard rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the standard rate` IS A NUMBER @desc What age do the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the standard rate? (years) (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I know the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the enhanced rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the enhanced rate` IS A NUMBER @desc What age do the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the enhanced rate? (years) (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `my ability to carry out mobility activities is limited by my physical or mental condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the assessment find that your ability to carry out mobility activities is limited by your physical or mental condition?
      `my ability to carry out mobility activities is severely limited by my physical or mental condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the assessment find that your ability to carry out mobility activities is severely limited by your physical or mental condition?
      `I know whether I meet the required period condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I meet the required period condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you meet the "required period condition"? (The question under section 81 asks what it turns on.) (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I suffer from a progressive disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you suffer from a progressive disease?
      `the date I ask about` IS A DATE @desc On what date do you ask whether you are terminally ill? (day, month and year)
      `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected` IS A DATE @desc By what date can your death in consequence of the disease reasonably be expected? (If it is not expected in the foreseeable future, give a date far ahead, such as 1 January 2100.)
      `I have made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground of terminal illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground that you are terminally ill?
      `I have reached pensionable age within the meaning of the Pensions Act 1995` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you reached pensionable age, as the rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the Pensions Act 1995 work it out?
      `I know whether regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations give an exception to the bar on entitlement after the relevant age that covers you? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I know whether regulations bar my mobility component for the period because I am unlikely to benefit from enhanced mobility` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations bar my mobility component for the period because I am unlikely to benefit from enhanced mobility` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide that you are not entitled to the mobility component for the period, because during all or most of it you are unlikely to benefit from enhanced mobility? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 79(6), am I entitled to the mobility component at the enhanced rate` IS
    `s 79(6) the person is entitled to the mobility component, subject to the other provisions of Part 4 relating to entitlement`
        `my result at the enhanced rate`
        (LIST `s 77(3) the person is not barred for want of the prescribed residence and presence conditions` `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set, as far as I know`,
              `s 77(4) the person is not barred by being entitled to a Scottish disability payment` `the Scottish disability payments I am entitled to`,
              `s 83(1) the person is not barred by having reached the relevant age` `me` `I have reached pensionable age within the meaning of the Pensions Act 1995` `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me, as far as I know`,
              `s 79(7) the person is not barred by regulations about unlikelihood of benefiting from enhanced mobility` `regulations bar my mobility component for the period because I am unlikely to benefit from enhanced mobility, as far as I know`)
    WHERE
        `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set` `I meet the residence and presence conditions the regulations set`
        `the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the standard rate, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a number the person may be unable to give` `I know the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the standard rate` `the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the standard rate`
        `the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the enhanced rate, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a number the person may be unable to give` `I know the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the enhanced rate` `the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the enhanced rate`
        `I meet the required period condition, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I meet the required period condition` `I meet the required period condition`
        `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me` `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me`
        `regulations bar my mobility component for the period because I am unlikely to benefit from enhanced mobility, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations bar my mobility component for the period because I am unlikely to benefit from enhanced mobility` `regulations bar my mobility component for the period because I am unlikely to benefit from enhanced mobility`
        `the Scottish disability payments I am entitled to` MEANS
            concat (LIST (IF `I am entitled to Scottish adult disability living allowance` THEN LIST `s 95 Scottish adult disability living allowance` ELSE EMPTY),
                         (IF `I am entitled to pension age disability payment` THEN LIST `s 95 pension age disability payment` ELSE EMPTY),
                         (IF `I am entitled to adult disability payment` THEN LIST `s 95 adult disability payment` ELSE EMPTY),
                         (IF `I am entitled to child disability payment` THEN LIST `s 95 child disability payment` ELSE EMPTY))
        `me` MEANS Person WITH
                `reference` IS "claimant"                    -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads the reference
                `age in years` IS `my age in years`
                `date of birth` IS Date 1 1 1900              -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads the date of birth
                `where the person is` IS `Greater London`     -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads where the person is
                `where the person lives` IS `Greater London`  -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads where the person lives
        `I am terminally ill at the date I ask about` MEANS
            `s 82(4) the person is terminally ill at the time` `I suffer from a progressive disease` `the date I ask about` `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected`
        `section 82 applies to me` MEANS
            `s 82(1) this section applies to the person` `I am terminally ill at the date I ask about` `I have made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground of terminal illness`
        `the required period condition limb as it stands for me` MEANS
            `s 82(3) the required period condition limb of section 79(1)(c) and (2)(c), as it stands for the person` `section 82 applies to me` `I meet the required period condition, as far as I know`
        `my result at the standard rate` MEANS
            `s 79(1) the person is entitled to the mobility component at the standard rate` `my ability to carry out mobility activities is limited by my physical or mental condition`
                (`s 79(1)(a) the person is of or over the age prescribed` `me` `the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the standard rate, as far as I know`) `the required period condition limb as it stands for me`
        `my result at the enhanced rate` MEANS
            `s 79(2) the person is entitled to the mobility component at the enhanced rate` `my ability to carry out mobility activities is severely limited by my physical or mental condition`
                (`s 79(2)(a) the person is of or over the age prescribed` `me` `the age the regulations prescribe for the mobility component at the enhanced rate, as far as I know`) `the required period condition limb as it stands for me`
        `my own result under section 79(1) or (2)` MEANS
            `s 78(6) the disjunction of the results` (LIST `my result at the standard rate`, `my result at the enhanced rate`)

§§ `Section 80 -- Ability to carry out daily living activities or mobility activities`

@desc Only section 80(5)(a), and only if regulations use the power. JUST TRUE means a negative determination is treated as made against you; NOTHING means the text does not say (including when regulations have not been settled). It is never a no.
@export Under section 80(5)(a), is a negative determination treated as made against me?
GIVEN `I know whether regulations treat a negative determination as made if a person fails without a good reason to comply with a requirement under section 80(4)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations treat a negative determination as made if a person fails without a good reason to comply with a requirement under section 80(4)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide that a negative determination is treated as made if a person fails, without a good reason, to comply with a requirement to give information or evidence or to take part in a consultation? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I failed to comply with a requirement to give information or evidence or to take part in a consultation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to comply with a requirement to give information or evidence, or to take part in a consultation or attend one?
      `I had a good reason for the failure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have a good reason for the failure?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 80(5)(a), is a negative determination treated as made against me` IS
    `s 80(5)(a) a negative determination is treated as made against the person` `regulations treat a negative determination as made if a person fails without a good reason to comply with a requirement under section 80(4), as far as I know` `I failed to comply with a requirement to give information or evidence or to take part in a consultation` `I had a good reason for the failure`
    WHERE
        `regulations treat a negative determination as made if a person fails without a good reason to comply with a requirement under section 80(4), as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations treat a negative determination as made if a person fails without a good reason to comply with a requirement under section 80(4)` `regulations treat a negative determination as made if a person fails without a good reason to comply with a requirement under section 80(4)`

§§ `Section 81 -- Required period condition: further provision`

@desc The usual test only (previous 3 months and next 9 months). Regulations may change the test in prescribed cases (section 81(4)); the next question asks about that.
@export Under section 81(1), do I meet the required period condition on the usual test?
GIVEN `at every time in the previous 3 months an assessment would likely have found my ability limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it likely that, as respects every time in the previous 3 months, an assessment at that time would have found your ability to carry out the activities limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)?
      `at every time in the next 9 months an assessment would likely find my ability limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it likely that, as respects every time in the next 9 months, an assessment at that time would find your ability to carry out the activities limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 81(1), do I meet the required period condition on the usual test` IS
    `s 81(1) the person meets the required period condition on the usual test` `at every time in the previous 3 months an assessment would likely have found my ability limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)` `at every time in the next 9 months an assessment would likely find my ability limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)`

@desc The usual test or, in a case regulations prescribe, the regulations' own provision. NOTHING means whether regulations apply to your case has not been settled, or their result for your case has not.
@export Under section 81(4), do I meet the required period condition?
GIVEN `at every time in the previous 3 months an assessment would likely have found my ability limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it likely that, as respects every time in the previous 3 months, an assessment at that time would have found your ability to carry out the activities limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)?
      `at every time in the next 9 months an assessment would likely find my ability limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it likely that, as respects every time in the next 9 months, an assessment at that time would find your ability to carry out the activities limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)?
      `I know whether regulations determine my case otherwise than under section 81(1) to (3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations determine my case otherwise than under section 81(1) to (3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide that, in your case, the required period condition is determined otherwise than by the usual test? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I know whether the result under the regulations' provision for my case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `the result under the regulations' provision for my case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If regulations determine your case otherwise, do you meet the required period condition under their provision for your case? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 81(4), do I meet the required period condition` IS
    `s 81(4) the person meets the required period condition` `regulations determine my case otherwise than under section 81(1) to (3), as far as I know` `my result on the usual test` `the result under the regulations' provision for my case, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `regulations determine my case otherwise than under section 81(1) to (3), as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations determine my case otherwise than under section 81(1) to (3)` `regulations determine my case otherwise than under section 81(1) to (3)`
        `the result under the regulations' provision for my case, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether the result under the regulations' provision for my case` `the result under the regulations' provision for my case`
        `my result on the usual test` MEANS
            `s 81(1) the person meets the required period condition on the usual test` `at every time in the previous 3 months an assessment would likely have found my ability limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)` `at every time in the next 9 months an assessment would likely find my ability limited (or severely limited, as the case may be)`

§§ `Section 82 -- Terminal illness`

@desc The section 82(4) definition only. The twelve months run from the date you ask about and end the day before the same date a year on.
@export Under section 82(4), am I terminally ill on the date I ask about?
GIVEN `I suffer from a progressive disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you suffer from a progressive disease?
      `the date I ask about` IS A DATE @desc On what date do you ask whether you are terminally ill? (day, month and year)
      `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected` IS A DATE @desc By what date can your death in consequence of the disease reasonably be expected? (If it is not expected in the foreseeable future, give a date far ahead, such as 1 January 2100.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 82(4), am I terminally ill on the date I ask about` IS
    `s 82(4) the person is terminally ill at the time` `I suffer from a progressive disease` `the date I ask about` `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected`

@desc Whether section 82 applies to you: terminally ill and a claim made expressly on that ground. NOTHING means the section says nothing about you (it applies only to a person who is both).
@export Under section 82(1), does section 82 apply to me?
GIVEN `I suffer from a progressive disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you suffer from a progressive disease?
      `the date I ask about` IS A DATE @desc On what date do you ask whether you are terminally ill? (day, month and year)
      `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected` IS A DATE @desc By what date can your death in consequence of the disease reasonably be expected? (If it is not expected in the foreseeable future, give a date far ahead, such as 1 January 2100.)
      `I have made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground of terminal illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground that you are terminally ill?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 82(1), does section 82 apply to me` IS
    `s 82(1) this section applies to the person` `I am terminally ill on the date I ask about` `I have made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground of terminal illness`
    WHERE
        `I am terminally ill on the date I ask about` MEANS
            `s 82(4) the person is terminally ill at the time` `I suffer from a progressive disease` `the date I ask about` `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected`

@desc Only the section 82(2) grant. NOTHING means section 82 does not apply to you, so this section says nothing; section 78 may still entitle you (see the question under section 78(6)).
@export Under section 82(2), am I entitled to the daily living component at the enhanced rate because section 82 applies to me?
GIVEN `I suffer from a progressive disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you suffer from a progressive disease?
      `the date I ask about` IS A DATE @desc On what date do you ask whether you are terminally ill? (day, month and year)
      `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected` IS A DATE @desc By what date can your death in consequence of the disease reasonably be expected? (If it is not expected in the foreseeable future, give a date far ahead, such as 1 January 2100.)
      `I have made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground of terminal illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground that you are terminally ill?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 82(2), am I entitled to the daily living component at the enhanced rate because section 82 applies to me` IS
    `s 82(2) the person is entitled to the daily living component at the enhanced rate` `section 82 applies to me`
    WHERE
        `I am terminally ill on the date I ask about` MEANS
            `s 82(4) the person is terminally ill at the time` `I suffer from a progressive disease` `the date I ask about` `the date by which my death in consequence of the disease can reasonably be expected`
        `section 82 applies to me` MEANS
            `s 82(1) this section applies to the person` `I am terminally ill on the date I ask about` `I have made a claim for personal independence payment expressly on the ground of terminal illness`

@desc Only the section 82(5) rule. The knowledge or authority of the person does not matter. NOTHING means the rule does not reach the case.
@export Under section 82(5), is the person for whom a claim was made on their behalf regarded as making the claim?
GIVEN `someone purports to make a claim for personal independence payment on behalf of another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did someone purport to make a claim for personal independence payment on behalf of another person?
      `the claim is made expressly on the ground that the person for whom it purports to be made is terminally ill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the claim made expressly on the ground that the person for whom it purports to be made is terminally ill?
      `the claim is made without that person's knowledge or authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the claim made without the knowledge or authority of the person for whom it purports to be made?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 82(5), is the person for whom a claim was made on their behalf regarded as making the claim` IS
    `s 82(5) the person on whose behalf the claim purports to be made is regarded as making the claim` `someone purports to make a claim for personal independence payment on behalf of another` `the claim is made expressly on the ground that the person for whom it purports to be made is terminally ill` `the claim is made without that person's knowledge or authority`

§§ `Section 83 -- Persons of pensionable age`

@desc Only the section 83(1) bar. The relevant age is pensionable age or, if higher, 65. JUST FALSE means you are barred; NOTHING means an exception in regulations has not been settled.
@export Under section 83(1), am I free of any bar from having reached the relevant age?
GIVEN `my age in years` IS A NUMBER @desc How old are you, in completed years?
      `I have reached pensionable age within the meaning of the Pensions Act 1995` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you reached pensionable age, as the rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the Pensions Act 1995 work it out?
      `I know whether regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations give an exception to the bar on entitlement after the relevant age that covers you? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 83(1), am I free of any bar from having reached the relevant age` IS
    `s 83(1) the person is not barred by having reached the relevant age` `me` `I have reached pensionable age within the meaning of the Pensions Act 1995` `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me` `regulations give an exception to the bar at the relevant age that covers me`
        `me` MEANS Person WITH
                `reference` IS "claimant"                    -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads the reference
                `age in years` IS `my age in years`
                `date of birth` IS Date 1 1 1900              -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads the date of birth
                `where the person is` IS `Greater London`     -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads where the person is
                `where the person lives` IS `Greater London`  -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads where the person lives

§§ `Section 84 -- No entitlement to daily living component where UK is not competent state`

@desc Only the section 84(1) bar on the daily living component.
@export Under section 84(1), am I free of any bar from the United Kingdom not being the competent state?
GIVEN `a relevant EU Regulation applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does Council Regulation (EC) No 1408/71 or Regulation (EC) No 883/2004 apply to you?
      `the United Kingdom is competent to pay me sickness benefits in cash for the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc During the period, is the United Kingdom the competent state for paying you sickness benefits in cash (for the purposes of Chapter 1 of Title III of the Regulation that applies)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 84(1), am I free of any bar from the United Kingdom not being the competent state` IS
    `s 84(1) the person is not barred from the daily living component by the United Kingdom not being the competent state` `a relevant EU Regulation applies to me` `the United Kingdom is competent to pay me sickness benefits in cash for the period`

§§ `Section 85 -- Care home residents`

@desc Only the section 85(1) bar on payment, and only if regulations use the power. JUST FALSE means no amount attributable to the daily living component is payable for the period; NOTHING means the regulations have not been settled.
@export Under section 85(1), is the amount attributable to my daily living component free of the care home bar?
GIVEN `I know whether I am a resident of a care home` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I am a resident of a care home` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a resident of a care home (an establishment that provides accommodation together with nursing or personal care)? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `any of the costs of qualifying services provided for me are borne out of public or local funds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are any of the costs of the qualifying services (accommodation, board, personal care) provided for you borne out of public or local funds?
      `I know whether the costs are borne by virtue of a specified enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `the costs are borne by virtue of a specified enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are those costs borne by virtue of an enactment that regulations specify? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I know whether regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the care home condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the care home condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide that no amount attributable to the daily living component is payable for a period when you meet the care home condition? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 85(1), is the amount attributable to my daily living component free of the care home bar` IS
    `s 85(1) the amount attributable to the daily living component is not barred as payable` `I meet the condition in section 85(2)` `regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the care home condition, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `I am a resident of a care home, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I am a resident of a care home` `I am a resident of a care home`
        `the costs are borne by virtue of a specified enactment, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether the costs are borne by virtue of a specified enactment` `the costs are borne by virtue of a specified enactment`
        `regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the care home condition, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the care home condition` `regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the care home condition`
        `I meet the condition in section 85(2)` MEANS
            `s 85(2) the person meets the condition` `I am a resident of a care home, as far as I know` `any of the costs of qualifying services provided for me are borne out of public or local funds` `the costs are borne by virtue of a specified enactment, as far as I know`

§§ `Section 86 -- Hospital in-patients`

@desc Only the section 86(1) bar on payment, and only if regulations use the power for that component. JUST FALSE means no amount attributable to that component is payable for the period; NOTHING means the regulations have not been settled.
@export Under section 86(1), is the amount attributable to my component free of the hospital in-patient bar?
GIVEN `the component` IS A `Component of personal independence payment` @desc Which component do you ask about? (daily living component or mobility component)
      `I am undergoing medical or other treatment as an in-patient at a hospital or similar institution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you undergoing medical or other treatment as an in-patient at a hospital or similar institution?
      `I know whether any of the costs of my treatment, accommodation and related services are borne out of public funds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `any of the costs of my treatment, accommodation and related services are borne out of public funds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are any of the costs of your treatment, accommodation and related services borne out of public funds? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I know whether regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide that no amount attributable to the daily living component is payable for a period when you meet the hospital in-patient condition? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I know whether regulations bar the mobility component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations bar the mobility component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide that no amount attributable to the mobility component is payable for a period when you meet the hospital in-patient condition? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 86(1), is the amount attributable to my component free of the hospital in-patient bar` IS
    `s 86(1) the amount attributable to the component is not barred as payable` `the component` `I meet the condition in section 86(2)` `regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition, as far as I know` `regulations bar the mobility component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `any of the costs of my treatment, accommodation and related services are borne out of public funds, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether any of the costs of my treatment, accommodation and related services are borne out of public funds` `any of the costs of my treatment, accommodation and related services are borne out of public funds`
        `regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition` `regulations bar the daily living component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition`
        `regulations bar the mobility component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations bar the mobility component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition` `regulations bar the mobility component while I meet the hospital in-patient condition`
        `I meet the condition in section 86(2)` MEANS
            `s 86(2) the person meets the condition` `I am undergoing medical or other treatment as an in-patient at a hospital or similar institution` `any of the costs of my treatment, accommodation and related services are borne out of public funds, as far as I know`

§§ `Section 87 -- Prisoners and detainees`

@desc Only the section 87 bar. JUST FALSE means no amount is payable for the period; NOTHING means whether regulations provide otherwise has not been settled.
@export Under section 87, am I free of the bar on payment during imprisonment or detention?
GIVEN `I am undergoing imprisonment or detention in legal custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you undergoing imprisonment or detention in legal custody?
      `I know whether regulations provide otherwise, to the extent that covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `regulations provide otherwise, to the extent that covers me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide that an amount is payable despite your imprisonment or detention, in a way that covers you? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 87, am I free of the bar on payment during imprisonment or detention` IS
    `s 87 the amount is not barred as payable by imprisonment or detention` `I am undergoing imprisonment or detention in legal custody` `regulations provide otherwise, to the extent that covers me, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `regulations provide otherwise, to the extent that covers me, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether regulations provide otherwise, to the extent that covers me` `regulations provide otherwise, to the extent that covers me`

§§ `Section 88 -- Claims, awards and information`

@desc Only the section 88(1) bar, tested on the first day of the period.
@export Under section 88(1), am I free of the bar on payment for a period before my claim?
GIVEN `the first day of the period` IS A DATE @desc What is the first day of the period you ask about? (day, month and year)
      `the date on which a claim for personal independence payment was made or treated as made for me` IS A DATE @desc On what date was a claim for personal independence payment made, or treated as made, by you or on your behalf?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 88(1), am I free of the bar on payment for a period before my claim` IS
    `s 88(1) the person is not barred for a period by its beginning before the claim` `the first day of the period` `the date on which a claim for personal independence payment was made or treated as made for me`

@desc Only the section 88(2) rule. JUST FALSE means the person making the award considers a fixed term inappropriate, so the rule does not require one.
@export Under section 88(2), must my award be for a fixed term?
GIVEN `the person making my award considers that a fixed term award would be inappropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person making your award consider that a fixed term award would be inappropriate?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 88(2), must my award be for a fixed term` IS
    `s 88(2) the award is required to be for a fixed term` `the person making my award considers that a fixed term award would be inappropriate`

§§ `Section 90 -- Abolition of disability living allowance`

@desc Only whether the benefit is disability living allowance, which those sections provided for. NOTHING means the section says nothing about the benefit you chose.
@export Under section 90, is this benefit abolished by the repeal of sections 71 to 76 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992?
GIVEN `the benefit` IS A `Benefit` @desc Which benefit do you ask about?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 90, is this benefit abolished by the repeal of sections 71 to 76 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992` IS
    `s 90 the benefit is abolished by the repeal of sections 71 to 76 of the Contributions and Benefits Act` `the benefit`

§ `Part 6 -- Miscellaneous`

§§ `Section 135 -- Functions of registration service`

@desc Section 19A(1) of the Registration Service Act 1953, as inserted. NOTHING means the text does not say (it states what the functions include, not what they exclude).
@export Under section 135, do the functions of a registrar include the power to transmit and verify the information about my birth for the Secretary of State?
GIVEN `the information is in a declaration made under section 9(1) of the Births and Deaths Registration Act 1953 or entered in a register of births` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the information about your birth contained in a declaration made under section 9(1) of the Births and Deaths Registration Act 1953, or entered in a register of births?
      `the transmission or verification is for the purposes of the Tell Us Once service operated by the Secretary of State` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the information being transmitted or verified for the purposes of the service operated by the Secretary of State through which individuals transmit information about births and that information may be passed to other persons?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 135, do the functions of a registrar include the power to transmit and verify the information about my birth for the Secretary of State` IS
    `s 135 RSA 19A(1) the functions of a registrar include the power to transmit and verify the information for the Secretary of State` `the information is in a declaration made under section 9(1) of the Births and Deaths Registration Act 1953 or entered in a register of births` `the transmission or verification is for the purposes of the Tell Us Once service operated by the Secretary of State`

@desc Section 19A(4) of the Registration Service Act 1953, as inserted. JUST FALSE means the section does not authorise a disclosure that is unlawful on either ground; NOTHING means the section does not say.
@export Under section 135, does the section authorise this disclosure?
GIVEN `the disclosure is unlawful by virtue of an enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the disclosure be unlawful by virtue of any enactment?
      `the disclosure is unlawful by reason of the law of confidentiality or privacy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the disclosure be unlawful by reason of the law relating to confidentiality or privacy?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 135, does the section authorise this disclosure` IS
    `s 135 RSA 19A(4) the section authorises the disclosure` `the disclosure is unlawful by virtue of an enactment` `the disclosure is unlawful by reason of the law of confidentiality or privacy`

§§ `Section 136 -- Supporting maintenance agreements`

@desc Section 9(2A)(b) of the Child Support Act 1991, as inserted: an invitation before an application is accepted. NOTHING means the section does not say.
@export Under section 136(1), may the Secretary of State invite me to consider whether it is possible to make a maintenance agreement?
GIVEN `my application under section 4 or 7 of the Child Support Act 1991 has been accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your application for child support maintenance under section 4 or 7 of the Child Support Act 1991 been accepted?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 136(1), may the Secretary of State invite me to consider whether it is possible to make a maintenance agreement` IS
    `s 136(1) CSA 9(2A)(b) the Secretary of State may invite the applicant to consider whether it is possible to make a maintenance agreement` `my application under section 4 or 7 of the Child Support Act 1991 has been accepted`

@desc Paragraph 3(3) of Schedule 5 to the Child Maintenance and Other Payments Act 2008, as inserted. NOTHING means the paragraph does not say.
@export Under section 136(2), may the Secretary of State invite me to consider whether it is possible to make a maintenance agreement before accepting my application in a transferred case?
GIVEN `my application is one required by provision under paragraph 3(2)(b) of Schedule 5 to the Child Maintenance and Other Payments Act 2008` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application one that is required by provision under paragraph 3(2)(b) of Schedule 5 to the Child Maintenance and Other Payments Act 2008 (a case transferred to the new rules)?
      `my application under section 4 or 7 of the Child Support Act 1991 has been accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your application for child support maintenance under section 4 or 7 of the Child Support Act 1991 been accepted?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 136(2), may the Secretary of State invite me to consider whether it is possible to make a maintenance agreement before accepting my application in a transferred case` IS
    `s 136(2) CMOPA Sch 5 para 3(3) the Secretary of State may invite the applicant to consider whether it is possible to make a maintenance agreement` `my application is one required by provision under paragraph 3(2)(b) of Schedule 5 to the Child Maintenance and Other Payments Act 2008` `my application under section 4 or 7 of the Child Support Act 1991 has been accepted`

§§ `Section 137 -- Collection of child support maintenance`

@desc Section 4(2A) of the Child Support Act 1991, as inserted: the Secretary of State "may only" make arrangements if one of two conditions holds. JUST FALSE means neither holds; NOTHING means a condition holds, which the section states as necessary, not sufficient.
@export Under section 137(2), may the Secretary of State make arrangements under section 4(2)(a) of the Child Support Act 1991 for the collection of my child support maintenance?
GIVEN `the non-resident parent agrees to the arrangements` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the non-resident parent agree to the collection arrangements?
      `the Secretary of State is satisfied that without the arrangements child support maintenance is unlikely to be paid in accordance with the calculation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Secretary of State satisfied that, without the arrangements, child support maintenance is unlikely to be paid in accordance with the calculation?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 137(2), may the Secretary of State make arrangements under section 4(2)(a) of the Child Support Act 1991 for the collection of my child support maintenance` IS
    `s 137(2) CSA 4(2A) the Secretary of State may make arrangements under section 4(2)(a)` `the non-resident parent agrees to the arrangements` `the Secretary of State is satisfied that without the arrangements child support maintenance is unlikely to be paid in accordance with the calculation`

@desc Section 7(3A) of the Child Support Act 1991, as inserted: the Secretary of State "may only" make arrangements if one of two conditions holds. JUST FALSE means neither holds; NOTHING means a condition holds, which the section states as necessary, not sufficient.
@export Under section 137(3), may the Secretary of State make arrangements under section 7(3)(a) of the Child Support Act 1991 for the collection of maintenance for me as a child in Scotland?
GIVEN `the non-resident parent agrees to the arrangements` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the non-resident parent agree to the collection arrangements?
      `the Secretary of State is satisfied that without the arrangements child support maintenance is unlikely to be paid in accordance with the calculation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Secretary of State satisfied that, without the arrangements, child support maintenance is unlikely to be paid in accordance with the calculation?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 137(3), may the Secretary of State make arrangements under section 7(3)(a) of the Child Support Act 1991 for the collection of maintenance for me as a child in Scotland` IS
    `s 137(3) CSA 7(3A) the Secretary of State may make arrangements under section 7(3)(a)` `the non-resident parent agrees to the arrangements` `the Secretary of State is satisfied that without the arrangements child support maintenance is unlikely to be paid in accordance with the calculation`

@desc Section 29(1) of the Child Support Act 1991 as amended: subject to sections 4(2A) and 7(3A). JUST FALSE means neither condition holds; NOTHING means a condition holds (necessary, not sufficient) or the arrangement is of another kind.
@export Under section 137(4), may the Secretary of State make this arrangement for collection?
GIVEN `the arrangement` IS A `s 137(4) arrangement for collection` @desc Which arrangement is it? (an arrangement under section 4(2)(a), one under section 7(3)(a), or any other)
      `the non-resident parent agrees to the arrangements` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the non-resident parent agree to the collection arrangements?
      `the Secretary of State is satisfied that without the arrangements child support maintenance is unlikely to be paid in accordance with the calculation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Secretary of State satisfied that, without the arrangements, child support maintenance is unlikely to be paid in accordance with the calculation?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 137(4), may the Secretary of State make this arrangement for collection` IS
    `s 137(4) CSA 29(1) the Secretary of State may make the arrangement for collection, subject to sections 4(2A) and 7(3A)` `the arrangement` `the non-resident parent agrees to the arrangements` `the Secretary of State is satisfied that without the arrangements child support maintenance is unlikely to be paid in accordance with the calculation`

§§ `Section 138 -- Indicative maintenance calculations`

@desc Section 9A(1) of the Child Support Act 1991, as inserted: a person with care or non-resident parent in relation to a qualifying child. NOTHING means the section does not say.
@export Under section 138(1), may I apply for an indicative calculation?
GIVEN `my role in relation to the child` IS A `s 138 role in relation to a qualifying child` @desc What is your role in relation to the child? (a person with care, a non-resident parent, or neither)
      `the child is a qualifying child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the child a qualifying child?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 138(1), may I apply for an indicative calculation` IS
    `s 138 CSA 9A(1) the person may apply for an indicative calculation` `my role in relation to the child` `the child is a qualifying child`

@desc Section 9A(2) of the Child Support Act 1991, as inserted: a qualifying child aged 12 or over who is habitually resident in Scotland. NOTHING means the section does not say.
@export Under section 138(2), may I, a child, apply for an indicative calculation with respect to myself?
GIVEN `my age in years` IS A NUMBER @desc How old are you, in completed years?
      `I am a qualifying child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a qualifying child?
      `I am habitually resident in Scotland` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you habitually resident in Scotland?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 138(2), may I, a child, apply for an indicative calculation with respect to myself` IS
    `s 138 CSA 9A(2) the child may apply for an indicative calculation with respect to themselves` `me` `I am a qualifying child` `I am habitually resident in Scotland`
    WHERE
        `me` MEANS Person WITH
                `reference` IS "claimant"                    -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads the reference
                `age in years` IS `my age in years`
                `date of birth` IS Date 1 1 1900              -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads the date of birth
                `where the person is` IS `Greater London`     -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads where the person is
                `where the person lives` IS `Greater London`  -- Placeholder: no rule called here reads where the person lives

@desc Section 9A(3) of the Child Support Act 1991, as inserted. NOTHING means the definition is not met on the facts given; the text does not say what else a calculation might be.
@export Under section 138(3), is the calculation I was given an indicative calculation?
GIVEN `the calculation I was given is of the child support maintenance the Secretary of State considers would be fixed by a maintenance calculation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the calculation you were given a calculation of the amount of child support maintenance which the Secretary of State considers would, in accordance with section 11, be fixed by a maintenance calculation if one were made with respect to the child or children in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 138(3), is the calculation I was given an indicative calculation` IS
    `s 138 CSA 9A(3) the calculation is an indicative calculation` `the calculation I was given is of the child support maintenance the Secretary of State considers would be fixed by a maintenance calculation`

@desc Section 9A(4) of the Child Support Act 1991, as inserted. JUST FALSE means an indicative calculation creates no liability; NOTHING means the calculation is not shown to be an indicative calculation.
@export Under section 138(4), does my indicative calculation create a liability to pay child support maintenance?
GIVEN `the calculation I was given is an indicative calculation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the calculation you were given an indicative calculation?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 138(4), does my indicative calculation create a liability to pay child support maintenance` IS
    `s 138 CSA 9A(4) the calculation creates a liability to pay child support maintenance` `the calculation I was given is an indicative calculation`

@desc Section 9A(5) of the Child Support Act 1991, as inserted. NOTHING means the section gives the Secretary of State a power to set a limit and does not say what follows once the limit is reached.
@export Under section 138(5), is my application free of a bar from a limit on the number of applications?
GIVEN `the Secretary of State has set a limit on the number of applications in my case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Secretary of State set a limit on the number of applications that will be accepted in your case?
      `the limit the Secretary of State has set` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the limit? (0 if there is none)
      `the number of applications already accepted in my case` IS A NUMBER @desc How many applications have already been accepted in your case?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 138(5), is my application free of a bar from a limit on the number of applications` IS
    `s 138 CSA 9A(5) the application is not barred by a limit on the number of applications` `the Secretary of State has set a limit on the number of applications in my case` `the limit the Secretary of State has set` `the number of applications already accepted in my case`

@desc Section 9A(6) of the Child Support Act 1991, as inserted. JUST FALSE means the Secretary of State shall not make the calculation on that assumption.
@export Under section 138(6), is the Secretary of State free to make the indicative calculation on the assumption that the person is a parent?
GIVEN `the person alleged to be the parent denies being one of the child's parents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person alleged to be the parent deny being one of the child's parents?
      `the case falls within paragraph (b) of Case A3 in section 26(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the case fall within paragraph (b) of Case A3 in section 26(2) of the Child Support Act 1991?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 138(6), is the Secretary of State free to make the indicative calculation on the assumption that the person is a parent` IS
    `s 138 CSA 9A(6) the Secretary of State is not barred from making the calculation on the assumption that the person is a parent` `the person alleged to be the parent denies being one of the child's parents` `the case falls within paragraph (b) of Case A3 in section 26(2)`

§§ `Section 142 -- Exclusion from individual voluntary arrangements`

@desc Section 382(5) of the Insolvency Act 1986, as inserted. JUST FALSE means a liability to pay child support maintenance is not a debt or liability for Part 8; NOTHING means the subsection does not say about any other liability.
@export Under section 142(1), is my liability a debt or liability for the purposes of Part 8 of the Insolvency Act 1986 (individual voluntary arrangements)?
GIVEN `my liability is a liability under the Child Support Act 1991 to pay child support maintenance to a person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the liability a liability under the Child Support Act 1991 to pay child support maintenance to a person?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 142(1), is my liability a debt or liability for the purposes of Part 8 of the Insolvency Act 1986 (individual voluntary arrangements)` IS
    `s 142(1) IA 382(5) the liability is a debt or liability for the purposes of Part 8 of the Insolvency Act 1986` `my liability is a liability under the Child Support Act 1991 to pay child support maintenance to a person`

§§ `Section 144 -- Use of jobcentres by sex industry`

@desc Section 2A(2) of the Employment and Training Act 1973, as inserted: both limbs must hold. JUST FALSE means a limb fails.
@export Under section 144, is this employment employment for sexual purposes?
GIVEN `my employment involves the employee engaging in an activity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the employment involve the employee engaging in an activity?
      `the employee's activity, or the way it is performed, may reasonably be assumed to be intended solely or principally to stimulate others sexually` IS A BOOLEAN @desc May the employee's activity, or the way in which it is performed, reasonably be assumed to be intended solely or principally to stimulate one or more other persons sexually (by whatever means)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 144, is this employment employment for sexual purposes` IS
    `s 144 ETA 2A(2) the employment is employment for sexual purposes` `my employment involves the employee engaging in an activity` `the employee's activity, or the way it is performed, may reasonably be assumed to be intended solely or principally to stimulate others sexually`

@desc Section 2A(1) of the Employment and Training Act 1973, as inserted. JUST FALSE means the arrangements may not include it; NOTHING means whether an order makes an exception has not been settled.
@export Under section 144, may the Secretary of State's arrangements under section 2 of the Employment and Training Act 1973 include arrangements in respect of this employment?
GIVEN `my employment involves the employee engaging in an activity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the employment involve the employee engaging in an activity?
      `the employee's activity, or the way it is performed, may reasonably be assumed to be intended solely or principally to stimulate others sexually` IS A BOOLEAN @desc May the employee's activity, or the way in which it is performed, reasonably be assumed to be intended solely or principally to stimulate one or more other persons sexually (by whatever means)?
      `I know whether an order under section 2A(3) specifies an exception that covers the employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `an order under section 2A(3) specifies an exception that covers the employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does an order under section 2A(3) of the Employment and Training Act 1973 specify an exception to the restriction that covers the employment? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 144, may the Secretary of State's arrangements under section 2 of the Employment and Training Act 1973 include arrangements in respect of this employment` IS
    `s 144 ETA 2A(1) the arrangements under section 2 are not barred from including arrangements in respect of the employment` `the employment is for sexual purposes` `an order under section 2A(3) specifies an exception that covers the employment, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `an order under section 2A(3) specifies an exception that covers the employment, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether an order under section 2A(3) specifies an exception that covers the employment` `an order under section 2A(3) specifies an exception that covers the employment`
        `the employment is for sexual purposes` MEANS
            `s 144 ETA 2A(2) the employment is employment for sexual purposes` `my employment involves the employee engaging in an activity` `the employee's activity, or the way it is performed, may reasonably be assumed to be intended solely or principally to stimulate others sexually`

§ `Part 7 -- Final`

§§ `Section 150 -- Commencement`

@desc Section 150(3): the remaining provisions come into force on the day the Secretary of State appoints by order. NOTHING means no day has been appointed that you know of.
@export Under section 150(3), is the provision in force on my date?
GIVEN `the date I ask about` IS A DATE @desc On what date do you ask? (day, month and year)
      `I know the day appointed for the coming into force of the provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `the day appointed for the coming into force of the provision` IS A DATE @desc What day has been appointed for the coming into force of the provision? (day, month and year) (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 150(3), is the provision in force on my date` IS
    `s 150(3) the provision is in force on the date` `the date I ask about` `the day appointed for the coming into force of the provision, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `the day appointed for the coming into force of the provision, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a date the person may be unable to give` `I know the day appointed for the coming into force of the provision` `the day appointed for the coming into force of the provision`

§ `Schedule 4 -- State pension credit: housing credit (assumed, not ruled)`

@desc Section 3A(1) of the State Pension Credit Act 2002, as inserted (assumed, not ruled: housing credit is adjacent to the seat's core questions). JUST FALSE means a limb fails; NOTHING means a limb has not been settled.
@export Under schedule 4 paragraph 4, do I satisfy the first condition for housing credit?
GIVEN `I know whether I am liable to make payments in respect of the accommodation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I am liable to make payments in respect of the accommodation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you liable to make payments in respect of the accommodation you occupy as your home? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
      `I know whether I occupy the accommodation as my home` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
      `I occupy the accommodation as my home` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you occupy the accommodation as your home? (Ignored if you answered no to the question before this one.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under schedule 4 paragraph 4, do I satisfy the first condition for housing credit` IS
    `sch 4 para 4 SPCA 3A(1) the first condition for housing credit is met` `I am liable to make payments in respect of the accommodation, as far as I know` `I occupy the accommodation as my home, as far as I know`
    WHERE
        `I am liable to make payments in respect of the accommodation, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I am liable to make payments in respect of the accommodation` `I am liable to make payments in respect of the accommodation`
        `I occupy the accommodation as my home, as far as I know` MEANS
            `wra3 seat a yes or no the person may be unable to give` `I know whether I occupy the accommodation as my home` `I occupy the accommodation as my home`

@desc Section 3A(4) of the State Pension Credit Act 2002, as inserted (assumed, not ruled). Whether the accommodation is the whole or part of a building, and whether it is separate and self-contained, is immaterial.
@export Under schedule 4 paragraph 4, is my accommodation accommodation for the purposes of the housing credit conditions?
GIVEN `where my accommodation is` IS A `Place` @desc Where is the accommodation? (Greater London, England outside Greater London, Scotland, Wales, Northern Ireland, outside the United Kingdom)
      `my accommodation is residential accommodation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the accommodation residential accommodation?
      `my accommodation consists of the whole of a building` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the accommodation consist of the whole of a building?
      `my accommodation comprises separate and self-contained premises` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the accommodation comprise separate and self-contained premises?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under schedule 4 paragraph 4, is my accommodation accommodation for the purposes of the housing credit conditions` IS
    `sch 4 para 4 SPCA 3A(4) the accommodation is accommodation for the purposes of subsection (1)` `where my accommodation is` `my accommodation is residential accommodation` `my accommodation consists of the whole of a building` `my accommodation comprises separate and self-contained premises`

@desc Section 3A(2) of the State Pension Credit Act 2002, as inserted (assumed, not ruled): the amount payable must not be less than the prescribed amount. NOTHING means the prescribed amount has not been settled.
@export Under schedule 4 paragraph 4, do I satisfy the second condition for housing credit?
GIVEN `the amount of the housing credit that would be payable if I were entitled to it` IS A NUMBER @desc What amount of housing credit would be payable to you if you were entitled to it? (pounds)
      `I know the amount regulations prescribe for the purposes of section 3A(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can you answer the next question yet? Answer no if the regulations or a decision-maker have not settled it for you, or you do not know.
