GIVEN `the body that made the decision` IS A `sch 12 body` @desc Which body made the decision? (an appeal tribunal constituted under Chapter 1 of Part 1 of the Social Security Act 1998; a person appointed as a Child Support Commissioner; a person appointed as a Social Security Commissioner; a tribunal of Social Security Commissioners; or any other body)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under schedule 12 paragraph 3, is my decision within the decisions open to supersession (Child Support Act 1991, Schedule 4C paragraph 2)` IS
    `sch 12 para 3 CSA Sch 4C para 2 the decision is within the decisions listed` `the body that made the decision`

§§ `Paragraph 4 -- Social Security Act 1998, section 10`

@desc Only the added limb. JUST TRUE means the decision is one the amendment adds to the decisions open to supersession; NOTHING means the amendment adds one limb to a list whose other limbs are in the host Act, which is not among the sources.
@export Under schedule 12 paragraph 4, is my decision within the decisions open to supersession (Social Security Act 1998, section 10(1)(aa))?
GIVEN `the body that made the decision` IS A `sch 12 body` @desc Which body made the decision? (an appeal tribunal constituted under Chapter 1 of Part 1 of the Social Security Act 1998; a person appointed as a Child Support Commissioner; a person appointed as a Social Security Commissioner; a tribunal of Social Security Commissioners; or any other body)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under schedule 12 paragraph 4, is my decision within the decisions open to supersession (Social Security Act 1998, section 10(1)(aa))` IS
    `sch 12 para 4(2) SSA1998 10(1)(aa) the decision is within the decisions listed as open to supersession` `the body that made the decision`

§§ `Paragraph 5 -- Child Support, Pensions and Social Security Act 2000, Schedule 7`

@desc Only the added limb. JUST TRUE means the decision is one the amendment adds to the decisions open to supersession; NOTHING means the amendment adds one limb to a list whose other limbs are in the host Act, which is not among the sources.
@export Under schedule 12 paragraph 5, is my decision within the decisions open to supersession (Child Support, Pensions and Social Security Act 2000, Schedule 7 paragraph 4(1)(aa))?
GIVEN `the body that made the decision` IS A `sch 12 body` @desc Which body made the decision? (an appeal tribunal constituted under Chapter 1 of Part 1 of the Social Security Act 1998; a person appointed as a Child Support Commissioner; a person appointed as a Social Security Commissioner; a tribunal of Social Security Commissioners; or any other body)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under schedule 12 paragraph 5, is my decision within the decisions open to supersession (Child Support, Pensions and Social Security Act 2000, Schedule 7 paragraph 4(1)(aa))` IS
    `sch 12 para 5 CSPSSA Sch 7 para 4(1)(aa) the decision is within the decisions listed as open to supersession` `the body that made the decision`

-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part wra4 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-04; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- SEAT (layer 2).  The person asking is "a person who claims, receives or is moved to Universal Credit,
-- or who claims PIP, or whose benefits are capped".
-- Every question below is named "Under section N," and calls the layer-1 rule for that provision; it restates nothing.
-- A question that layer 1 answers with a MAYBE answers with a MAYBE here:
--   JUST TRUE / JUST FALSE is the text answering; NOTHING is the text not covering the case (never read it as FALSE).
-- A layer-1 rule that returns a plain BOOLEAN is wrapped in JUST, so every yes/no question here has one answer shape.
-- Where a layer-1 rule takes a record, the record is built in a WHERE from facts the person is asked,
-- and a field the rule does not read gets a placeholder with a comment saying so.


§ `Sections 96 to 97: the benefit cap`

-- A person whose benefits are capped asks what the cap does to them: whether it can bite, by how much, at what limit, and whether the decision can be appealed.

@export Under section 96(1), can a benefit cap be applied to my welfare benefits?
GIVEN `regulations provide for a benefit cap` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide for a benefit cap to be applied to the benefits of a person in your position? Leave out if not known.
      `regulations except the person from the cap` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do the regulations make an exception that covers you? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 96(1), a benefit cap can be applied to my welfare benefits` MEANS
    `s 96(1) a benefit cap is applied to the person's welfare benefits` `regulations provide for a benefit cap` `regulations except the person from the cap`

@export Under section 96(2), what is the most the benefit cap could reduce my welfare benefits by?
GIVEN `the total entitlement` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the total, in pounds, of the welfare benefits you are entitled to for the reference period?
      `the relevant amount` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc What is the relevant amount for the reference period under the benefit cap regulations? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`Under section 96(2), the most the cap can reduce my welfare benefits by` MEANS
    `s 96(2) the greatest reduction the cap can require` `the total entitlement` `the relevant amount`

@export Under section 96(2), does my total entitlement to welfare benefits exceed the relevant amount?
GIVEN `the total entitlement` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the total, in pounds, of the welfare benefits you are entitled to for the reference period?
      `the relevant amount` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the relevant amount, in pounds, for that reference period under the benefit cap regulations?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 96(2), my total entitlement to welfare benefits exceeds the relevant amount` MEANS
    JUST (`s 96(2) the total entitlement exceeds the relevant amount` `the total entitlement` `the relevant amount`)

@export Under section 96(5A), what is the annual limit that applies to me?
GIVEN `the person is resident in Greater London as regulations define resident` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do you live in Greater London, in the sense of "resident" that the benefit cap regulations define? Leave out if not known.
      `the figure regulations specify for the person` IS A MAYBE `s 96(5A) figure of a pair` @desc Which figure of the pair do the regulations specify for you (the first or the second)? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`Under section 96(5A), the annual limit that applies to me` MEANS
    `s 96(5A) the annual limit` `the person is resident in Greater London as regulations define resident` `the figure regulations specify for the person`

@export Under section 96(10), am I a single person for the purposes of the benefit cap?
GIVEN `the person is a member of a couple in the sense of s 96(10)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Are you a member of a couple, as the benefit cap regulations describe a couple? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 96(10), I am a single person for the purposes of the benefit cap` MEANS
    `s 96(10) the person is a single person` `the person is a member of a couple in the sense of s 96(10)`

@export Under section 96A(1), is the five-year review of the benefit cap sums overdue?
GIVEN `the date of the last review` IS A DATE @desc On what date did the Secretary of State last review the sums for the benefit cap?
      `the day in question` IS A DATE @desc On what day are you asking the question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 96A(1), the five-year review of the sums is overdue` MEANS
    JUST (`s 96A(1) the five-year review is overdue` `the date of the last review` `the day in question`)

-- The decision record is built here from what the person is asked.
-- The decision-maker, the benefit and the date are not read by the rule (it turns on the kind of decision only).
@export Under section 97(6), can I appeal against a decision to apply the benefit cap?
GIVEN `the kind of decision` IS A `Kind of decision` @desc What kind of decision are you asking about (for example a decision to apply the benefit cap, or a decision on entitlement to a benefit or on its amount)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 97(6), an appeal lies against the decision to apply the benefit cap` MEANS
    `s 97(6) SSA1998 Sch 2 para 8A an appeal lies against the decision` decision
    WHERE
        decision MEANS Decision WITH
            `the kind of decision` IS `the kind of decision`,
            `the decision-maker` IS `the Secretary of State`,   -- placeholder: not read by the rule
            `the benefit concerned` IS `universal credit`,       -- placeholder: not read by the rule
            `the date it was made` IS Date 1 1 2025              -- placeholder: not read by the rule


§ `Sections 99 to 102: claims, payments and appeals`

@export Under section 99(3), can regulations require me to supply information?
GIVEN `the person is of a description regulations prescribe` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Are you of a description that the regulations prescribe as one who must supply information? Leave out if not known.
      `the information is or could be relevant to a claim or award, or to potential claims or awards` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the information one that is, or could be, relevant to a claim or award of the benefit, or to a possible later claim or award?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 99(3), regulations can require me to supply information` MEANS
    `s 99(3) SSAA 5(1A) regulations may require the person to supply the information` `the person is of a description regulations prescribe` `the information is or could be relevant to a claim or award, or to potential claims or awards`

@export Under section 101(1), can regulations provide for a payment on account to me?
GIVEN `it is impracticable for a claim to be made or determined, or an award determined or paid in full, immediately` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it impracticable for your claim to be made or decided immediately, or for your award to be decided or paid in full immediately?
      `the case is one of need` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you in need?
      `the Secretary of State considers, in accordance with prescribed criteria, that the payment can reasonably be expected to be recovered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Secretary of State consider, by the prescribed criteria, that a payment on account to you can reasonably be expected to be recovered?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 101(1), regulations can provide for a payment on account to me` MEANS
    `s 101(1) SSAA 5(1)(r) regulations may provide for a payment on account in this case` `it is impracticable for a claim to be made or determined, or an award determined or paid in full, immediately` `the case is one of need` `the Secretary of State considers, in accordance with prescribed criteria, that the payment can reasonably be expected to be recovered`

@export Under section 102(2), do I have a right of appeal because regulations give me one?
GIVEN `regulations under subsection (3A) so provide` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations under SSA 1998 s 12(3A) give you a right of appeal in your case? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 102(2), I have a right of appeal because regulations give me one` MEANS
    `s 102(2) SSA1998 12(2)(b) there is a right of appeal because regulations so provide` `regulations under subsection (3A) so provide`

@export Under section 102(3), do I have a right of appeal against the decision?
GIVEN `the regulations require consideration of revision before an appeal` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations require the Secretary of State to have considered revising the decision before you can appeal it? Leave out if not known.
      `the Secretary of State has considered whether to revise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Secretary of State considered whether to revise the decision (SSA 1998 s 9)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 102(3), I have a right of appeal against the decision` MEANS
    `s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3A) there is a right of appeal` `the regulations require consideration of revision before an appeal` `the Secretary of State has considered whether to revise`

@export Under section 102(4), can my purported appeal be treated as an application for revision?
GIVEN `there is no right of appeal in accordance with regulations under subsection (3A)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the regulations say you have no right of appeal against this decision?
      `regulations provide for a purported appeal to be treated as an application for revision` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide that your purported appeal is treated as an application for revision? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 102(4), my purported appeal can be treated as an application for revision` MEANS
    `s 102(4) SSA1998 12(7)(b) the purported appeal may be treated as an application for revision` `there is no right of appeal in accordance with regulations under subsection (3A)` `regulations provide for a purported appeal to be treated as an application for revision`


§ `Section 105: recovering an overpayment`

-- Section 105 inserts sections 71ZB to 71ZH into the Social Security Administration Act 1992 (SSAA).

@export Under section 105, inserting SSAA 71ZB(1)(a), how much universal credit was I paid in excess of my entitlement?
GIVEN `s 3 the claimant is entitled to universal credit for the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you entitled to universal credit for the period?
      `s 8(1) the amount of the award of universal credit for the period` IS A NUMBER @desc What was the amount of universal credit you were entitled to for the period, in pounds?
      `the amount paid` IS A NUMBER @desc How much universal credit was paid to you for the period, in pounds?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`Under section 105, SSAA 71ZB(1)(a) fixes the universal credit I was paid in excess of my entitlement` MEANS
    `s 105 SSAA 71ZB(1)(a) the universal credit paid in excess of entitlement` `s 3 the claimant is entitled to universal credit for the period` `s 8(1) the amount of the award of universal credit for the period` `the amount paid`

@export Under section 105, inserting SSAA 71ZB(1), may the Secretary of State recover an overpayment of this benefit?
GIVEN `the benefit` IS A Benefit @desc Which benefit was overpaid?
      `prescribed circumstances apply to the housing credit` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc If the benefit is housing credit: do the prescribed circumstances apply that stop an overpayment of it being recovered? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 105, SSAA 71ZB(1) lets the Secretary of State recover an overpayment of this benefit` MEANS
    `s 105 SSAA 71ZB(1) the Secretary of State may recover an overpayment of the benefit` `the benefit` `prescribed circumstances apply to the housing credit`

@export Under section 105, inserting SSAA 71ZB(2), is the amount recoverable from me?
GIVEN `the person is the person to whom the amount was paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the amount paid to you?
      `regulations prescribe the person as one from whom it is recoverable` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations prescribe you as another person from whom the amount is recoverable? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 105, SSAA 71ZB(2) makes the amount recoverable from me` MEANS
    `s 105 SSAA 71ZB(2) the amount is recoverable from the person` `the person is the person to whom the amount was paid` `regulations prescribe the person as one from whom it is recoverable`

@export Under section 105, inserting SSAA 71ZB(3), does the decision I was paid under not bar recovery?
GIVEN `the amount was paid in pursuance of a determination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the amount paid because of a decision (a "determination") about your entitlement?
      `the determination has been reversed or varied on an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has that decision been reversed or varied on an appeal?
      `the determination has been revised or superseded under section 9 or 10 of the Social Security Act 1998` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has that decision been revised or superseded (SSA 1998 ss 9 and 10)?
      `regulations otherwise provide` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide that the amount is recoverable even though the decision has not been reversed, varied, revised or superseded? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 105, SSAA 71ZB(3) means the decision does not bar recovery` MEANS
    `s 105 SSAA 71ZB(3) the determination does not bar recovery` `the amount was paid in pursuance of a determination` `the determination has been reversed or varied on an appeal` `the determination has been revised or superseded under section 9 or 10 of the Social Security Act 1998` `regulations otherwise provide`

@export Under section 105, inserting SSAA 71ZD(3)(e), what is the most that may be deducted from my earnings?
GIVEN `the earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc What are your earnings for the period, in pounds?
      `the level of earnings below which the regulations forbid reducing earnings` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc What is the level of earnings, in pounds, below which the regulations say your earnings must not be reduced? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`Under section 105, SSAA 71ZD(3)(e) limits what can be deducted from my earnings` MEANS
    `s 105 SSAA 71ZD(3)(e) the most that may be deducted from the earnings` `the earnings` `the level of earnings below which the regulations forbid reducing earnings`

@export Under section 105, inserting SSAA 71ZH(1), may the Secretary of State recover this payment from me?
GIVEN `the kind of payment` IS A `s 105 SSAA 71ZH(1) kind of payment` @desc What kind of payment is it (a universal credit hardship payment, a jobseeker's allowance hardship payment, a jobseeker's allowance paid at a prescribed rate, or another listed payment)?
      `s 28(2)(f) the hardship payment is recoverable under section 28` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the universal credit hardship payment one that is recoverable under section 28 of the Act (the regulations say whether hardship payments must be repaid)?
      `s 46 JSA 19C(2)(f) the hardship payment is recoverable under section 19C` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the jobseeker's allowance hardship payment one that is recoverable under section 19C of the Jobseekers Act 1995?
      `the allowance is payable at a prescribed rate under paragraph 9` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the jobseeker's allowance paid at a rate prescribed under paragraph 9 of Schedule 1 to the Jobseekers Act 1995?
      `s 105 SSAA 71ZH(1)(c) the payment is recoverable under paragraph 9 of Schedule 1 to the Jobseekers Act 1995` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the payment recoverable under paragraph 9 of Schedule 1 to the Jobseekers Act 1995?
      `s 105 SSAA 71ZH(1)(d) the payment is recoverable under paragraph 10 of Schedule 1 to the Jobseekers Act 1995` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the payment recoverable under paragraph 10 of Schedule 1 to the Jobseekers Act 1995?
      `s 105 SSAA 71ZH(1)(e) the payment is recoverable under the Social Security Fraud Act 2001 provisions listed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the payment recoverable under the Social Security Fraud Act 2001 provisions listed in section 71ZH(1)(e)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 105, SSAA 71ZH(1) lets the Secretary of State recover this payment` MEANS
    `s 105 SSAA 71ZH(1) the Secretary of State may recover the payment` `the kind of payment` `s 28(2)(f) the hardship payment is recoverable under section 28` `s 46 JSA 19C(2)(f) the hardship payment is recoverable under section 19C` `the allowance is payable at a prescribed rate under paragraph 9` `s 105 SSAA 71ZH(1)(c) the payment is recoverable under paragraph 9 of Schedule 1 to the Jobseekers Act 1995` `s 105 SSAA 71ZH(1)(d) the payment is recoverable under paragraph 10 of Schedule 1 to the Jobseekers Act 1995` `s 105 SSAA 71ZH(1)(e) the payment is recoverable under the Social Security Fraud Act 2001 provisions listed`

@export Under section 105, inserting SSAA 115B(4)(a), is the penalty recoverable from me?
GIVEN `the recipient has agreed, in the specified manner, to pay the penalty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you agreed, in the manner the notice specified, to pay the penalty?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 105, SSAA 115B(4)(a) makes the penalty recoverable from me` MEANS
    `s 105(4) SSAA 115B(4)(a) the penalty is recoverable from the recipient` `the recipient has agreed, in the specified manner, to pay the penalty`

@export Under section 105, inserting SSAA 115B(4)(b), may criminal proceedings be instituted against me for the conduct?
GIVEN `the recipient has agreed, in the specified manner, to pay the penalty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you agreed, in the manner the notice specified, to pay the penalty?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 105, SSAA 115B(4)(b) allows criminal proceedings against me for the conduct` MEANS
    `s 105(4) SSAA 115B(4)(b) criminal proceedings may be instituted against the recipient for the conduct` `the recipient has agreed, in the specified manner, to pay the penalty`

@export Under section 105, inserting SSA1998 Schedule 3 paragraph 6B, can I appeal against a decision on how much I must repay?
GIVEN `the decision is as to the amount of payment recoverable under section 71ZB, 71ZG or 71ZH` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the decision about how much you must repay under section 71ZB, 71ZG or 71ZH of the Administration Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 105, Schedule 3 paragraph 6B gives me an appeal against the decision on the amount to repay` MEANS
    `s 105(7) SSA1998 Sch 3 para 6B an appeal lies against the decision` `the decision is as to the amount of payment recoverable under section 71ZB, 71ZG or 71ZH`


§ `Sections 106 to 112: earnings, investigation and prosecution`

@export Under section 106(1), can an amount I owe be recovered by deduction from my earnings?
GIVEN `the amount is recoverable under a provision mentioned in SSAA section 71(8)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the amount one that is recoverable under a provision mentioned in section 71(8) of the Administration Act (overpayments in general)?
      `regulations provide for deductions from earnings` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations provide for such amounts to be recovered by deductions from earnings? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 106(1), an amount I owe can be recovered by deduction from my earnings` MEANS
    `s 106(1) SSAA 71(9A) the amount may be recovered by deduction from earnings` `the amount is recoverable under a provision mentioned in SSAA section 71(8)` `regulations provide for deductions from earnings`

@export Under section 110(a), can I be required to supply information for an investigation?
GIVEN `the person is of a description regulations prescribe` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Are you of a description that the regulations prescribe as one who may be required to give information for an investigation? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 110(a), I can be required to supply information for an investigation` MEANS
    `s 110(a) SSAA 109B(2)(ia) the person may be required to supply information for an investigation` `the person is of a description regulations prescribe`

@export Under section 112(2), can the local authority bring proceedings against me for the benefit offence?
GIVEN `where the proceedings would be brought` IS A Place @desc Where would the proceedings be brought?
      `the authority administers housing benefit or council tax benefit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the authority bringing the proceedings administer housing benefit or council tax benefit?
      `the authority has already started an investigation of this offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the authority already started an investigation into this offence?
      `the proceedings relate to housing benefit and the authority has already investigated or prosecuted a council tax benefit offence by the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the proceedings are about housing benefit: has the authority already started an investigation, or brought proceedings, about a council tax benefit offence by you?
      `the proceedings relate to council tax benefit and the authority has already investigated or prosecuted a housing benefit offence by the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the proceedings are about council tax benefit: has the authority already started an investigation, or brought proceedings, about a housing benefit offence by you?
      `a direction under subsection (3) says the authority may not bring them despite (a), (b) or (c) being met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Secretary of State directed that the authority may not bring these proceedings even though it had already started an investigation?
      `the proceedings arise in prescribed circumstances or are of a prescribed description` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do the proceedings arise in circumstances, or are they of a description, that the regulations prescribe? Leave out if not known.
      `the Secretary of State has directed that the authority may bring the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Secretary of State directed that the authority may bring these proceedings?
      `a condition prescribed under subsection (5) is not satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Secretary of State prescribed a condition for bringing these proceedings that is not satisfied?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 112(2), the authority can bring proceedings against me for the benefit offence` MEANS
    `s 112(2) SSAA 116ZA(2) the authority may bring proceedings for the benefit offence` `where the proceedings would be brought` `the authority administers housing benefit or council tax benefit` `the authority has already started an investigation of this offence` `the proceedings relate to housing benefit and the authority has already investigated or prosecuted a council tax benefit offence by the person` `the proceedings relate to council tax benefit and the authority has already investigated or prosecuted a housing benefit offence by the person` `a direction under subsection (3) says the authority may not bring them despite (a), (b) or (c) being met` `the proceedings arise in prescribed circumstances or are of a prescribed description` `the Secretary of State has directed that the authority may bring the proceedings` `a condition prescribed under subsection (5) is not satisfied`


§ `Sections 113 to 116: penalties`

@export Under section 113(2), does the penalty provision also apply to me?
GIVEN `it appears that there are grounds for instituting proceedings for an offence relating to an act or omission in relation to any benefit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it appear to the Secretary of State or an authority that there are grounds for proceedings against you for an offence about an act or omission of yours in relation to a benefit?
      `where an overpayment, had one been made, would have been recoverable` IS A `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) provision under which the overpayment would have been recoverable` @desc If you had been overpaid because of that act or omission, under which section of the Administration Act would the overpayment have been recoverable (71, 71ZB, 71A, 75 or 76), or some other provision?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 113(2), the penalty provision also applies to me` MEANS
    `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A) the penalty provision also applies` `it appears that there are grounds for instituting proceedings for an offence relating to an act or omission in relation to any benefit` `where an overpayment, had one been made, would have been recoverable`

@export Under section 114(1), what is the amount of my penalty?
GIVEN `the amount of the overpayment, in pounds` IS A NUMBER @desc How much were you overpaid, in pounds?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`Under section 114(1), the amount of my penalty` MEANS
    `s 114(1) SSAA 115A(3) the amount of the penalty` `the amount of the overpayment, in pounds`

@export Under section 115(2), did I withdraw my agreement to pay the penalty within the cooling-off period?
GIVEN `the number of days from the agreement to pay the penalty until its withdrawal` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after you agreed to pay the penalty did you withdraw your agreement (0 if the same day)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 115(2), I withdrew my agreement to pay the penalty within the cooling-off period` MEANS
    `s 115(2) SSAA 115A(5) the agreement is withdrawn within the cooling-off period` `the number of days from the agreement to pay the penalty until its withdrawal`

@export Under section 116(1), inserting SSAA 115C(1), does the penalty for a negligent error apply to me?
GIVEN `the person negligently made an incorrect statement or gave incorrect information in or in connection with a claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you negligently make an incorrect statement, or give incorrect information or evidence, in or in connection with a claim for the benefit?
      `the person negligently made an incorrect statement or gave incorrect information in connection with an award` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you negligently make an incorrect statement, or give incorrect information or evidence, in connection with an award of the benefit?
      `the person failed to take reasonable steps to correct the error` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to take reasonable steps to correct the error?
      `the error resulted in the making of an overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the error result in your being overpaid?
      `the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been charged with an offence, cautioned, or given a penalty notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 116(1), SSAA 115C(1) applies to me` MEANS
    `s 116(1) SSAA 115C(1) the section applies` `the person negligently made an incorrect statement or gave incorrect information in or in connection with a claim` `the person negligently made an incorrect statement or gave incorrect information in connection with an award` `the person failed to take reasonable steps to correct the error` `the error resulted in the making of an overpayment` `the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment`

@export Under section 116(1), inserting SSAA 115D(1), can a penalty be imposed on me for failing to provide information?
GIVEN `the person failed to provide information or evidence as the appropriate authority required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to provide information or evidence that the authority required of you in connection with a claim for, or an award of, a benefit?
      `the person had a reasonable excuse for the failure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have a reasonable excuse for the failure?
      `the failure resulted in the making of an overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the failure result in your being overpaid?
      `the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been charged with an offence, cautioned, or given a penalty notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 116(1), a penalty can be imposed on me for failing to provide information` MEANS
    `s 116(1) SSAA 115D(1) a penalty may be imposed for failing to provide information` `the person failed to provide information or evidence as the appropriate authority required` `the person had a reasonable excuse for the failure` `the failure resulted in the making of an overpayment` `the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment`

@export Under section 116(1), inserting SSAA 115D(2), can a penalty be imposed on me for failing to notify a change of circumstances?
GIVEN `the person failed to notify a relevant change of circumstances as required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to tell the authority about a change of circumstances that you were required to notify?
      `the person had a reasonable excuse for the failure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have a reasonable excuse for the failure?
      `the failure resulted in the making of an overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the failure result in your being overpaid?
      `the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been charged with an offence, cautioned, or given a penalty notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 116(1), a penalty can be imposed on me for failing to notify a change of circumstances` MEANS
    `s 116(1) SSAA 115D(2) a penalty may be imposed for failing to notify a change of circumstances` `the person failed to notify a relevant change of circumstances as required` `the person had a reasonable excuse for the failure` `the failure resulted in the making of an overpayment` `the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment`


§ `Sections 117 to 119: loss of benefit for a benefit offence`

-- These sections amend sections 6A, 6B and 7 of the Social Security Fraud Act 2001 (SSFA).

@export Under section 117(2), is this benefit a disqualifying benefit?
GIVEN `the benefit` IS A Benefit @desc Which benefit is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 117(2), this benefit is a disqualifying benefit` MEANS
    `s 117(2) SSFA 6A(1) the benefit is a disqualifying benefit` `the benefit`

-- The conviction record is built here from eight facts the person can give about their own conviction.
@export Under section 118(5), is my offence a relevant offence?
GIVEN `the place where the proceedings were brought` IS A Place @desc Where were the proceedings brought?
      `the offence is the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted of the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud (in England and Wales)?
      `the offence is a prescribed offence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence one that regulations prescribe as a relevant offence? Leave out if not known.
      `the offence was committed in the circumstances regulations prescribe` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Was the offence committed in circumstances that regulations prescribe? Leave out if not known.
      `the overpayment the court found the offence to relate to, in pounds` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc What overpayment, in pounds, did the court find the offence to relate to? Leave out if the court found none.
      `the sentence imposed` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) kind of sentence` @desc What kind of sentence was imposed (custodial, suspended, or some other)?
      `the length of the sentence imposed, in months` IS A NUMBER @desc How long was the sentence, in months?
      `the period the court found the offence to have been committed over, in months` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc Over how many months did the court find the offence to have been committed? Leave out if the court made no finding.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 118(5), the offence is a relevant offence` MEANS
    `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) the offence is a relevant offence` conviction
    WHERE
        conviction MEANS `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence` WITH
            `the place where the proceedings were brought` IS `the place where the proceedings were brought`,
            `the offence is the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud` IS `the offence is the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud`,
            `the offence is a prescribed offence` IS `the offence is a prescribed offence`,
            `the offence was committed in the circumstances regulations prescribe` IS `the offence was committed in the circumstances regulations prescribe`,
            `the overpayment the court found the offence to relate to, in pounds` IS `the overpayment the court found the offence to relate to, in pounds`,
            `the sentence imposed` IS `the sentence imposed`,
            `the length of the sentence imposed, in months` IS `the length of the sentence imposed, in months`,
            `the period the court found the offence to have been committed over, in months` IS `the period the court found the offence to have been committed over, in months`

-- Chained: the "relevant offence" input of SSFA 7(1A) is computed by the section 118(5) question from the eight facts about the conviction.
-- The rule says "the benefit offence referred to in section 7(1)(a), or any of them": this question is asked of one conviction; for several, ask Under section 118(5) of each.
@export Under section 118(7), do the restrictions in section 7 not apply to me?
GIVEN `the place where the proceedings were brought` IS A Place @desc Where were the proceedings brought?
      `the offence is the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted of the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud (in England and Wales)?
      `the offence is a prescribed offence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence one that regulations prescribe as a relevant offence? Leave out if not known.
      `the offence was committed in the circumstances regulations prescribe` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Was the offence committed in circumstances that regulations prescribe? Leave out if not known.
      `the overpayment the court found the offence to relate to, in pounds` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc What overpayment, in pounds, did the court find the offence to relate to? Leave out if the court found none.
      `the sentence imposed` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) kind of sentence` @desc What kind of sentence was imposed (custodial, suspended, or some other)?
      `the length of the sentence imposed, in months` IS A NUMBER @desc How long was the sentence, in months?
      `the period the court found the offence to have been committed over, in months` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc Over how many months did the court find the offence to have been committed? Leave out if the court made no finding.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 118(7), the restrictions in section 7 do not apply to me` MEANS
    `s 118(7) SSFA 7(1A) the restrictions in section 7 do not apply`
        (`Under section 118(5), the offence is a relevant offence` `the place where the proceedings were brought` `the offence is the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud` `the offence is a prescribed offence` `the offence was committed in the circumstances regulations prescribe` `the overpayment the court found the offence to relate to, in pounds` `the sentence imposed` `the length of the sentence imposed, in months` `the period the court found the offence to have been committed over, in months`)

@export Under section 119(5), are the conditions for a restriction under SSFA 7(1) met in my case?
GIVEN `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings?
      `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc On what date was each benefit offence committed?
      `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc On what date did each disqualifying event happen in relation to you?
      `the current set of proceedings has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section or section 8 or 9` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has this set of proceedings been left out of every earlier application of these sections to you or to anyone who was then a member of your family?
      `the earlier disqualifying event has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section or section 8 or 9` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the earlier disqualifying event been left out of every earlier application of these sections to you or to anyone who was then a member of your family?
      `the conditions for an entitlement to a sanctionable benefit are or become satisfied within the disqualification period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the conditions for an entitlement to a sanctionable benefit met for you, or do they become met, at any time within the disqualification period?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 119(5), the conditions for a restriction under SSFA 7(1) are met in my case` MEANS
    JUST (`s 119(5) SSFA 7(1) the conditions in paragraphs (a) to (e) are met` `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings` `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender` `the current set of proceedings has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section or section 8 or 9` `the earlier disqualifying event has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section or section 8 or 9` `the conditions for an entitlement to a sanctionable benefit are or become satisfied within the disqualification period`)

@export Under section 119(6), what is my disqualification period?
GIVEN `the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings` IS A DATE @desc On what date were you convicted in the current set of proceedings?
      `the prescribed date` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc What is the prescribed date on which the disqualification period begins? Leave out if not known.
      `the relevant period` IS A `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) relevant period` @desc Which relevant period applies to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`Under section 119(6), my disqualification period` MEANS
    `s 119(6) SSFA 7(6) the disqualification period` `the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings` `the prescribed date` `the relevant period`

@export Under section 119(8), inserting SSFA 7(7A), do the circumstances in which a restriction is treated as not imposed exist in my case?
GIVEN `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account in applying this section to the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been taken into account in applying this section to you?
      `the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been withdrawn under subsection (5) of the penalty provision?
      `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has it been decided on an appeal, or under social security regulations, that the overpayment is not recoverable or due?
      `the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the amount of the overpayment been revised on an appeal, or under social security regulations?
      `there is a new agreement to pay a penalty on the revised overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you agreed a new penalty on the revised overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 119(8), SSFA 7(7B) applies in my case` MEANS
    `s 119(8) SSFA 7(7A) subsection (7B) applies` `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account in applying this section to the person` `the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision` `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due` `the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations` `there is a new agreement to pay a penalty on the revised overpayment`

-- Chained: the "subsection (7B) applies" input of SSFA 7(7B) is computed by the section 119(8) question for 7(7A) from the five facts about the agreement.
@export Under section 119(8), inserting SSFA 7(7B), is the restriction to be treated as not imposed on me?
GIVEN `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account in applying this section to the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been taken into account in applying this section to you?
      `the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been withdrawn under subsection (5) of the penalty provision?
      `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has it been decided on an appeal, or under social security regulations, that the overpayment is not recoverable or due?
      `the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the amount of the overpayment been revised on an appeal, or under social security regulations?
      `there is a new agreement to pay a penalty on the revised overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you agreed a new penalty on the revised overpayment?
      `the restriction could have been imposed even if the person had not agreed to pay the penalty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Could the restriction have been imposed on you even if you had not agreed to pay the penalty?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 119(8), the restriction is to be treated as not imposed on me` MEANS
    `s 119(8) SSFA 7(7B) the restriction is to be treated as not imposed`
        (`Under section 119(8), SSFA 7(7B) applies in my case` `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account in applying this section to the person` `the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision` `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due` `the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations` `there is a new agreement to pay a penalty on the revised overpayment`)
        `the restriction could have been imposed even if the person had not agreed to pay the penalty`


§ `Sections 120 and 121: tax credits and cautions`

-- Section 120 inserts sections 36A to 36D into the Tax Credits Act 2002 (TCA); section 121 removes cautions as a ground.

@export Under section 120(2), inserting TCA 36A(1), does subsection (4) apply to me?
GIVEN `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in any proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you convicted of one or more benefit offences in any proceedings?
      `the offender agreed to pay a penalty as paragraph (b) describes` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Did you agree to pay a penalty in place of prosecution after being given a notice by an appropriate authority? Leave out if not known.
      `the offender is cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 120(2), TCA 36A(4) applies to me by virtue of 36A(1)` MEANS
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(1) subsection (4) applies by virtue of subsection (1)` `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in any proceedings` `the offender agreed to pay a penalty as paragraph (b) describes` `the offender is cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences`

@export Under section 120(2), inserting TCA 36B(1), is the restriction to be treated as not imposed on me?
GIVEN `the conviction is taken into account for the application of section 36A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your conviction taken into account in applying section 36A to you?
      `the conviction is subsequently quashed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the conviction since been quashed?
      `the restriction could have been imposed even if the conviction had not taken place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Could the restriction have been imposed on you even if the conviction had not taken place?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 120(2), TCA 36B(1) treats the restriction as not imposed on me` MEANS
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(1) the restriction is to be treated as not imposed` `the conviction is taken into account for the application of section 36A` `the conviction is subsequently quashed` `the restriction could have been imposed even if the conviction had not taken place`

@export Under section 120(3)(b), inserting TCA 38(1)(ca), can I appeal against the decision?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) kind of decision` @desc What kind of decision are you asking about?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 120(3)(b), I can appeal against the decision under TCA 38(1)(ca)` MEANS
    `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) an appeal lies against the decision` `the decision`

@export Under section 121(1)(c), is a caution a ground for the loss of my benefit?
GIVEN `the person is cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 121(1)(c), a caution is a ground for the loss of my benefit` MEANS
    `s 121(1)(c) SSFA 6B(1)(c) a caution is a ground for the loss of benefit` `the person is cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences`


§ `Sections 127 to 129: information held by HMRC and the Secretary of State`

-- Chained: "subsection (1) applies to the information" is computed by s 127(1) from what the information is held for and who holds it.
@export Under section 127(2), can information about me held for HMRC functions be supplied?
GIVEN `the information is held for the purposes of any HMRC functions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the information held for the purposes of any function of Her Majesty's Revenue and Customs?
      `the holder` IS A `s 127 holder of the information` @desc Who holds the information?
      `the recipient` IS A `s 127 recipient of the information` @desc To whom would the information be supplied?
      `the purpose` IS A `s 127 purpose of the use` @desc For what would the information be used?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 127(2), information about me held for HMRC functions can be supplied` MEANS
    `s 127(2) information held for HMRC functions may be supplied`
        (`s 127(1) subsection (1) applies to the information` `the information is held for the purposes of any HMRC functions` `the holder`)
        `the recipient` `the purpose`

-- Chained: "subsection (3) applies to the information" is computed by s 127(3) from what the information is held for and who holds it.
@export Under section 127(4), can information about me held for departmental functions be supplied?
GIVEN `the information is held for the purposes of any departmental functions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the information held for the purposes of a departmental function (social security, employment or training, tax credit prosecutions, child support)?
      `the holder` IS A `s 127 holder of the information` @desc Who holds the information?
      `the recipient` IS A `s 127 recipient of the information` @desc To whom would the information be supplied?
      `the purpose` IS A `s 127 purpose of the use` @desc For what would the information be used?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 127(4), information about me held for departmental functions can be supplied` MEANS
    `s 127(4) information held for departmental functions may be supplied`
        (`s 127(3) subsection (3) applies to the information` `the information is held for the purposes of any departmental functions` `the holder`)
        `the recipient` `the purpose`

@export Under section 128(1), can the Secretary of State supply information about me?
GIVEN `the supplier` IS A `s 128 supplier` @desc Who would supply the information?
      `the kind of information` IS A `s 128 kind of information` @desc Is the information social security information or child support information?
      `the person` IS A `s 128(2) person` @desc Who would the information be supplied to?
      `the purpose` IS A `s 128(3) purpose` @desc For what would the information be used?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 128(1), the Secretary of State can supply information about me` MEANS
    `s 128(1) the Secretary of State may supply the information` `the supplier` `the kind of information` `the person` `the purpose`

-- The composite question calls the four lower rules and hands their answers to the section 129(1) rule.
@export Under section 129(1), is the person who received my information barred from disclosing it?
GIVEN `the information was supplied to a person under section 128` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the information about you supplied to a person under section 128?
      `the discloser is that person, or an employee or former employee of that person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person who would disclose it that person, or an employee or former employee of that person?
      `the information relates to a particular person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the information relate to a particular person (you, or someone else)?
      `the disclosure` IS A `s 129(2) kind of disclosure` @desc What kind of disclosure is it (for example a summary that identifies no one, a disclosure for the Director of Public Prosecutions, for a criminal investigation, ordered by a court, or made with the consent of everyone the information relates to)?
      `the disclosure contravenes a restriction imposed by the Director of Public Prosecutions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the disclosure contravene a restriction imposed by the Director of Public Prosecutions?
      `the information relates to schemes and arrangements under section 2 of the Employment and Training Act 1973` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the information relate to schemes and arrangements under section 2 of the Employment and Training Act 1973?
      `another Act, or an instrument made under an Act, provides otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does another Act, or an instrument made under an Act, provide otherwise for this disclosure?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 129(1), the bar on disclosure applies to the disclosure of my information` MEANS
    `s 129(1) the bar on disclosure applies to the disclosure`
        (`s 129(1) subsection (1) bars the disclosure` `the information was supplied to a person under section 128` `the discloser is that person, or an employee or former employee of that person` `the information relates to a particular person`)
        (`s 129(2) subsection (1) does not apply to the disclosure` `the disclosure` `the disclosure contravenes a restriction imposed by the Director of Public Prosecutions`)
        (`s 129(3) subsection (1) does not apply to the information` `the information relates to schemes and arrangements under section 2 of the Employment and Training Act 1973`)
        (`s 129(4) subsection (1) stands unaffected by any other enactment` `another Act, or an instrument made under an Act, provides otherwise`)

-- Chained: the "bar applies" input of s 129(5) is computed by the section 129(1) question from the seven facts about the information and the disclosure.
@export Under section 129(5), does the person who disclosed my information commit an offence?
GIVEN `the information was supplied to a person under section 128` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the information about you supplied to a person under section 128?
      `the discloser is that person, or an employee or former employee of that person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person who would disclose it that person, or an employee or former employee of that person?
      `the information relates to a particular person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the information relate to a particular person (you, or someone else)?
      `the disclosure` IS A `s 129(2) kind of disclosure` @desc What kind of disclosure is it (for example a summary that identifies no one, a disclosure for the Director of Public Prosecutions, for a criminal investigation, ordered by a court, or made with the consent of everyone the information relates to)?
      `the disclosure contravenes a restriction imposed by the Director of Public Prosecutions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the disclosure contravene a restriction imposed by the Director of Public Prosecutions?
      `the information relates to schemes and arrangements under section 2 of the Employment and Training Act 1973` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the information relate to schemes and arrangements under section 2 of the Employment and Training Act 1973?
      `another Act, or an instrument made under an Act, provides otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does another Act, or an instrument made under an Act, provide otherwise for this disclosure?
      `the person made the disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person you are asking about make the disclosure?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 129(5), the person who disclosed my information commits an offence` MEANS
    `s 129(5) the person commits an offence`
        (`Under section 129(1), the bar on disclosure applies to the disclosure of my information` `the information was supplied to a person under section 128` `the discloser is that person, or an employee or former employee of that person` `the information relates to a particular person` `the disclosure` `the disclosure contravenes a restriction imposed by the Director of Public Prosecutions` `the information relates to schemes and arrangements under section 2 of the Employment and Training Act 1973` `another Act, or an instrument made under an Act, provides otherwise`)
        `the person made the disclosure`


§ `Sections 130 to 132: information held by local authorities`

-- Chained: "section 130 applies" is computed by s 130(1) (with s 130(2) for the kind of information), and "the recipient is a person specified in (6)" by s 130(6).
@export Under section 130(5), can the local authority use or supply information about me?
GIVEN `a local authority holds the information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a local authority hold the information?
      `the information` IS A `s 130(2) kind of information` @desc What is the information about (the fact of the service, when it begins or ends, or other information about the service and its funding)?
      `the information is prescribed` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Do regulations prescribe the information? Leave out if not known.
      `the person is receiving or is likely to receive a relevant service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you receiving, or likely to receive, overnight care at home, a residential care service or overnight hospital accommodation?
      `what the local authority does` IS A `s 130(5) what the local authority does` @desc What would the local authority do with the information about you (use it itself, supply it to another person, or something else)?
      `the purposes relate to the payment of a relevant benefit to the individual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the purposes ones relating to the payment of a relevant benefit to you?
      `the person to be given the information` IS A `s 130(6) person` @desc Who would be given the information (the Secretary of State, a person providing services to the Secretary of State, a local authority, a person authorised by a local authority about a relevant benefit, a person providing services about a relevant benefit to a local authority, or someone else)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 130(5), the local authority can use or supply information about me` MEANS
    `s 130(5) the local authority may use or supply the information`
        (`s 130(1) this section applies` `a local authority holds the information` (`s 130(2) the information falls within subsection (2)` `the information` `the information is prescribed`) `the person is receiving or is likely to receive a relevant service`)
        `what the local authority does` `the purposes relate to the payment of a relevant benefit to the individual`
        (`s 130(6) the person is a person specified in subsection (6)` `the person to be given the information`)

-- Chained: "the recipient is a qualifying person" is computed by s 131(11) from the description of the recipient.
@export Under section 131(1), can the Secretary of State supply information about me to a qualifying person?
GIVEN `the supplier is the Secretary of State or a person providing services to the Secretary of State` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the supplier the Secretary of State, or a person providing services to the Secretary of State?
      `the information is relevant information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the information about a relevant social security benefit or about welfare services?
      `the person` IS A `s 131(11) description of person` @desc Which description fits the person who would be given the information (a local authority, a person authorised by or providing services to a local authority about welfare services or council tax, an authority that administers housing benefit or a person authorised by or providing services to one, a person the Secretary of State prescribes, or someone else)?
      `the Secretary of State prescribes the person` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Has the Secretary of State prescribed that person, or a description that fits them? Leave out if not known.
      `the purpose is a prescribed purpose relating to welfare services or council tax` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN @desc Is the purpose one regulations prescribe for welfare services or council tax? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 131(1), the Secretary of State can supply information about me to a qualifying person` MEANS
    `s 131(1) the Secretary of State may supply the information to a qualifying person`
        `the supplier is the Secretary of State or a person providing services to the Secretary of State` `the information is relevant information`
        (`s 131(11) the person is a qualifying person` `the person` `the Secretary of State prescribes the person`)
        `the purpose is a prescribed purpose relating to welfare services or council tax`

-- Chained: "subsection (2) applies to the person" is computed by s 132(2) from the description of the person who disclosed.
@export Under section 132(1), is the person who disclosed my information guilty of an offence?
GIVEN `the person` IS A `s 132(2) description of person` @desc Which description fits the person who disclosed your information (a local authority or someone about welfare services or council tax, a provider of qualifying welfare services, a director or officer of one of those, an employee of one of those, or someone else)?
      `the person discloses the information without lawful authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did that person disclose the information without lawful authority?
      `the information comes to the person by virtue of section 131(1), (3) or (4)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the information come to that person by virtue of section 131(1), (3) or (4)?
      `the information relates to a particular person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the information relate to a particular person (you, or someone else)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 132(1), the person who disclosed my information is guilty of an offence` MEANS
    `s 132(1) the person is guilty of an offence`
        (`s 132(2) subsection (2) applies to the person` `the person`)
        `the person discloses the information without lawful authority` `the information comes to the person by virtue of section 131(1), (3) or (4)` `the information relates to a particular person`
